usage: writ traces <policy.json> [--depth N] [--max-states M] [--out <dir>]

Explore the policy breadth-first over a small alphabet of events and print, one JSON trace per
line, the shortest trace for every transition between abstract states, plus the first rejected
event found for each event type and reason. The policy may be layer 1 or 2; each trace carries
the layer-1 policy (for layer 2, what writ expand prints). The same input gives the same bytes.
  --depth N        events after session_start (default 4)
  --max-states M   stop after M abstract states (default 20000)
  --out <dir>      write <dir>/<name>.jsonl instead, <name> being the policy file name without .json

stderr gets states S, traces T (accepted A, rejected R), depth N, with ", truncated" when
--max-states stopped the search, after one line for each action left out because its name,
a guard name or an escalator name is longer than an event string may be.

Exit 0, or 2 for a usage, IO or policy error (a policy with check errors prints them).
